;;; DRAFT -- map!
;;; Edited 2026-08-03 by Priya.
;;; AUTHORED, not model-seeded. The drafting batch
;;; (scratch/pairs/draft_records.py, see draft_run.log) skipped
;;; this verb because an edited draft already existed, so no raw
;;; model output for it survives. Claiming model provenance here
;;; would be provenance nobody can check.
;;; Structure, examples, row programs, caveats and provenance are
;;; authored, not generated.
;;;
;;; Every :note is the literal placeholder PENDING-ENGINE. NOTHING in this
;;; file was run. The notes are filled by a deterministic script once the
;;; cons-cell engine lands. A printed value here would be a value nobody ran.
;;;
;;; Spec basis: ../spec-pack/map-bang.slatl -- read ../spec-pack/00-SOURCES.md first,
;;; the normative SRFI-1 text is NOT in this repo.

(verb
  :name "map!"
  :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "higher-order"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "state-change"
  :signature "(map! f lst) -> list"
  :summary "f applied to every element of lst, with the results written back into lst's own cells. The argument's contents are destroyed; its spine is not."
  :explanation "map! is the odd one in this family. Every other linear-update verb here re-points cdrs and leaves the elements alone; map! does the opposite -- it walks the spine writing a new car into every cell and changes no cdr at all. The suite makes the consequence explicit at list.sld:451-454: after (map! (lambda (x) (* x x)) l), both the returned list and l itself are (1 4 9). The spine is intact, the length is unchanged, and the returned list is eq? to the argument. The bang is a licence to destroy. After the call the cells that made up the argument have been re-used, so the variable you passed in no longer names what it named. Bind the result and read only that. But state the damage precisely, because it is not the usual damage: the argument variable still names a list of the right length in the right order, and every value in it has been replaced. Nothing about its shape says anything happened. Anyone else holding that list, or any cell of it, now sees mapped values where they left originals."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"
     :code "(define xs (list 1 2 3))\n(display (map! (lambda (n) (* n n)) xs))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate"
     :code ";; the argument is overwritten in place: xs holds the squares now.\n(define xs (list 1 2 3))\n(define m (map! (lambda (n) (* n n)) xs))\n(display (list 'm m 'xs xs 'same-object (eq? m xs)))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"
     :code ";; anyone else holding the list sees the new values, with no warning.\n(define xs (list 1 2 3))\n(define also-xs xs)\n(map! (lambda (n) (+ n 100)) xs)\n(display (list 'other-holder also-xs))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
  )
  :caveats ("The argument's CONTENTS are destroyed, not its spine. list.sld:451-454 asserts the argument itself equals the mapped result afterwards."
             "The result is eq? to the argument, the length is unchanged, and the shape gives no sign that anything was overwritten."
             "It writes cars, unlike every other linear-update verb in this set, which write cdrs. That is the difference to remember."
             "SRFI-1 defines the linear-update map over a single list. For the multi-list case use map."
             "f is applied for effect on the list as well as for its value; do not assume the order in which cells are written is unobservable.")
  :drawbacks ("It is the only verb here whose damage is invisible in the structure, which makes it the hardest to spot in review."
             "Any other holder of the list, or of a cell inside it, is silently affected."
             "It saves one allocation per element and nothing else; map is otherwise identical in cost.")
  :usecases ("Normalising a large freshly-built list in place inside a producer."
             "Scaling or quantising a private buffer of numbers where the originals are not wanted again."
             "Tight loops where the per-element allocation of map has been measured and matters.")
  :related ("map"
             "for-each"
             "filter!"
             "set-car!"
             "pair-for-each")
  :learn (:concept "Overwriting contents versus relinking structure"
          :prerequisites ("map" "set-car!" "set-cdr!" "eq?")
          :progression "Compare what map! writes with what filter! writes. One touches cars and one touches cdrs, and that single difference explains every difference in their hazards.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 linear-update procedures; conformance evidence scratch/r7rs-conformance/larceny/list.sld:451-454; signature derived from the map record in scheme/reference/33-higher-order.slat, restricted to the single-list case SRFI-1 specifies for the linear-update form. Drafted 2026-08-03; NOT RUN."
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define xs (list 1 2 3))\n(display (map! (lambda (n) (* n n)) xs))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define a (list 1 2 3))\n(define b (list 1 2 3))\n(display (equal? (map (lambda (n) (* n n)) a) (map! (lambda (n) (* n n)) b)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define xs (list 1 2 3))\n(define m (map! (lambda (n) (* n n)) xs))\n(display (list 'same-object (eq? m xs) 'argument-overwritten xs))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator wants to transform every element of a large list they built. Candidates: map (fresh list of the same length, source intact), for-each with an explicit set-car! (identical effect, and the mutation is visible at the call site), or map! (same effect, mutation hidden behind a familiar name). The middle option is worth real consideration: it is longer, and it is the only one of the three where a reader can see that a write happens."
     :explanation "eng: the transformed list comes back and the source variable holds the same transformed list. math: map! agrees with map on the single-list case for every f. cs: the argument is the result, so every holder of the list has been changed."
     :meta "When a destructive verb is spelled almost identically to a pure one and leaves no structural trace, the explicit longhand stops being verbosity and becomes documentation.")
    (:eng "Spelling the mutation out is worth a line when nothing else in the code shows it happened."
     :math "map and map! agree as functions; they differ only in whether a new spine is allocated."
     :cs "Structural invisibility is what makes this the hardest linear-update verb to catch in review.")
  )
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Replacing every entry of a sequence with a computed value, in the storage it already occupies."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(display (map! (lambda (n) (* 2 n)) (list 1 2 3)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "repainting every wagon in a rake" :code "(define rake (list 'red 'red 'red))\n(display (map! (lambda (w) 'blue) rake))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "revaluing every line of a price list" :code "(define prices (list 100 200 300))\n(display (map! (lambda (p) (* p 2)) prices))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, each car is overwritten where it sits. Repainting a rake changes every wagon and builds no new rolling stock; the rake is the same rake and the old colours are gone. Revaluing a price list in place changes what every line says without reissuing the document. All three share an update that preserves identity and destroys history."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever the container is meant to persist and only its contents are meant to change.")
    (:eng "Identity preserved, history destroyed. That is the trade in-place update always makes."
     :math "The mapping is pointwise, so the structure is untouched by definition and only the values move."
     :cs "This is why in-place transformation is safe for arrays and dangerous for shared lists: the danger comes from the sharing, not the writing.")
  )
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define xs (list 1 2 3))\n(define m (map! (lambda (n) (* n n)) xs))\n(display (list 'm m 'len (length m)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (length-preserved? f xs)\n  (= (length xs) (length (map f xs))))\n(display (list (length-preserved? (lambda (n) (* n n)) (list 1 2 3)) (length-preserved? (lambda (n) n) (list))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define xs (list 1 2 3))\n(define alias xs)\n(map! (lambda (n) (+ n 100)) xs)\n(display (list 'alias-sees-new-values alias))\n(newline)"
     :claim "For a proper list lst and a one-argument procedure f, map! returns a list eq? to lst whose elements are f applied to the original elements in order. The length and the spine are unchanged; every car has been replaced."
     :invariant "forall f, proper lst. eq?(map!(f, lst), lst) and |map!(f,lst)| = |lst| and equal?(map!(f,lst), map(f, lst-as-it-was))"
     :meta "The pattern is pointwise in-place update. What would falsify the claim: a fresh list returned, a changed length, or an argument left holding its original values. The alias program is the one that matters -- it is the only one that shows the damage, because the other two are equally true of map.")
    (:eng "Two of these three programs pass identically on map. Only the alias one is testing map!."
     :math "Pointwise transformation preserves length by construction; it is the cheapest invariant available."
     :cs "An eq? result plus changed contents is the exact signature of in-place update, and it is what a test must assert.")
  )
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (normalise! xs total) (map! (lambda (n) (/ n total)) xs))\n(display (normalise! (list 1 2 3) 6))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (compose-maps f g xs) (map f (map g xs)))\n(display (compose-maps (lambda (n) (+ n 1)) (lambda (n) (* n 2)) (list 1 2 3)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (map-via-for-each! f xs)\n  (pair-for-each (lambda (p) (set-car! p (f (car p)))) xs)\n  xs)\n(display (map-via-for-each! (lambda (n) (* n n)) (list 1 2 3)))\n(newline)"
     :base "map! writes f of each element back into the cell it came from."
     :emergent "eng: in-place normalisation, the numeric case this verb was made for. math: composing two maps is one map of the composition, and the fusion is available to the pure form and not to the destructive one, since the intermediate list is the argument. cs: writing map! out of pair-for-each and set-car! is four lines and shows exactly what it does, which is the version to prefer when a reader has to be convinced."
     :meta "The family is elementwise transformation. What emerges is that the pure form fuses and the destructive form does not, so a chain of two map! calls does twice the work a chain of two map calls needs to.")
    (:eng "The destructive form blocks the optimisation the pure form enables. Chained maps are the case where map wins outright."
     :math "map f after map g equals map of f-composed-with-g; that rewrite needs both maps to be pure."
     :cs "Building the verb from set-car! and pair-for-each is the readable version, and it is not meaningfully slower.")
  )
)
